#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087453 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbf50e27-a8c3-52e7-be21-5d7ed61e5512" :knowledge-point/title "Filling a Table of Values for a Parametric Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "53ce2176-ae44-578d-8fba-35c5018d4162" :question/math-academy-id "e-5075" :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n$x=3t, \\,\\,\\, y=t^3, \\quad -3\\leq t \\leq 3$,\n\nfill in the following table of values.\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=3t$. This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $-9$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we work out the $y$-values using $y=t^3$. This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $-9$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| $y$ | $-27$ | $-8$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $8$ | $27$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087457 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0dc11d5-8eda-52b1-b7b1-42b481813ff4" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Curve Defined Parametrically"}] :question/id #uuid "b322617c-ab21-5ce8-997f-2f10813b4280" :question/math-academy-id "e-5098" :question/problem "Plot the graph of the parametrically defined curve\n$x=t(t-6), \\,\\,\\, y=t^3, \\quad -2\\leq t \\leq 2$." :question/worked-solution "We need to create a table of values and then sketch the curve. First, we work out the $x$-values using the formula $x=t(t-6)$. This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $16$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-8$ |\n| $y$ |  |  |  |  |  |\n\nNow, we work out the $y$ values using $y=t^3$. This gives\n\n| **$t$** | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $16$ | $7$ | $0$ | $-5$ | $-8$ |\n| $y$ | $-8$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $8$ |\n\nPlotting these points, we obtain the following curve:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/803/Source/Images/e492a940fa95b406b60c6b0a5c438d98.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087458 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3cbf0ad0-a7c5-5c0f-a06c-9210fdc244ef" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Range of a Parametric Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "a792fa16-e1fd-5f30-a128-bbc49a285429" :question/math-academy-id "e-5099" :question/problem "Given that a curve is defined parametrically as\n$x=t^2-6t, \\,\\,\\, y=t^4+4, \\quad t \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$,\n\nwhat are the ranges of $x$ and $y$?" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider each variable in turn.\n\n- To find the range of $x$, we can start by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}x & = {t}^{2} - 6 t \\\\  & = {( t - 3 )}^{2} - ( 3 {)}^{2} \\\\  & = {( t - 3 )}^{2} - 9\\end{array}\n$$\n\nSince $\\left(t-3\\right)^2 \\geq 0$, we can conclude that $x \\geq -9$.\n- To find the range of $y=t^4+4$, notice that $t^4 \\geq 0$, and consequently $y \\geq 4$.\n\nTherefore, the ranges are\n\n$$\nx \\geq -9\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny \\geq 4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088212 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbf50e27-a8c3-52e7-be21-5d7ed61e5512" :knowledge-point/title "Filling a Table of Values for a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88abdcab-2d16-5df6-a394-86cb23753c8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086204} {:answer/id #uuid "9e4ba1e6-15fd-50c1-80e3-830ee7f16eeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086205} {:answer/id #uuid "a96cb040-c0c5-562e-99ff-a33f78af489d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086206} {:answer/id #uuid "69eb0c9c-575e-5911-badd-39d4243b48d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086207} {:answer/id #uuid "7d40b66e-4d25-5499-bb8e-5292bd261d23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88abdcab-2d16-5df6-a394-86cb23753c8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086204} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4ebc9cd-106a-5712-97de-c815929e4649" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086203}] :question/id #uuid "2efd117e-aa4e-5314-8b1b-8bd86f2a35c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-6834" :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n$x = t^{2} - 4, y = \\frac{5}{2} - \\frac{t}{2},-1 \\le t \\le 5$,\nconsider the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x$ | $\\textcolor{blue}{a}$ | $-4$ | $-3$ | $0$ | $5$ | $12$ | $21$ |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $\\frac{5}{2}$ | $\\textcolor{blue}{b}$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\textcolor{blue}{c}$ |\n\nFind the value of $a - b + c$."}]}
